= Threats to Validity <sec-threats>

*Internal.* All manual rubric scores reflect one evaluator's judgement; borderline cases (a 2 against a 3 on error recovery) are mitigated by explicit criteria at each level @kitchenham1997desmet, by the console presenting trace evidence beside the form, and by a free-text justification per score. The prompt templates were authored by the same evaluator, though their logical content is held constant across platforms. Language-model outputs are stochastic; the harness supports repeated runs with variance metrics, but the reported dataset was run at a single repeat.

*External.* Five of nine platforms are benchmarked, on one scenario, under two models; the visual platforms and the three harder scenarios are implemented but not run, so every finding is a programmatic-subset, basic-task baseline rather than a full-matrix result. In particular, a single scenario cannot distinguish a constant per-turn surcharge from a history-replay cost that compounds with task length, which is exactly the question the CrewAI and MAF profiles raise. Platform versions are recorded and findings are tied to them; agentic frameworks ship breaking changes between minor versions. The cross-model pass shows that one (model, provider) run is insufficient; it does not characterise model robustness.

*Construct.* Measuring the framework rather than the model is imperfect: platforms also differ in how they construct system prompts, replay history, and present tool results, and these shape output quality through the model. Two measurement caveats remain after the adapter audit. First, *iteration-count semantics differ* across adapters---LangGraph counts graph steps, CrewAI counts LLM calls, MAF counts executor turns, OpenAI SDK counts new items, and ADK counts authored events, inflating its count roughly 3--5× relative to peers---and CrewAI's outer retry loop multiplies its effective iteration budget by up to 3×; LangGraph's reviewer is bound to tools but has no tool-execution node, so its calls are dropped. Absolute per-platform numbers are interpretable; relative rankings on _Efficiency_ and _Orchestration_ carry the qualification that units are not uniform across adapter families. Second, *prompt wrapping is heterogeneous*: each adapter wraps the shared templates in its own role framing before the model sees them, so inter-platform differences blend orchestration with framework-level prompt construction. The 0--3 scale trades precision for reliability, and the aggregation constants (budgets, equal weighting) are design choices; raw per-stage metrics are retained so alternative aggregations can be computed.

*Conclusion.* No hypothesis tests are reported at $n = 1$. The primary claims---the replay-strategy taxonomy, the ≈2.4× cost spread, and the rtc separation---rest on effect sizes well above the within-platform variance observed during development; the complexity-scaling and model-amplification observations are presented as descriptive pending more data points.
